WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.578
Webhook security is the practice of safely receiving and processing event-
driven HTTP callbacks between systems.

2
00:00:06.578 --> 00:00:13.022
A provider sends an event to a consumer when something happens, and the
consumer decides what to do with that event.

3
00:00:13.022 --> 00:00:25.574
In Python, webhook handlers may live in Flask, Django, FastAPI, serverless
functions, background workers, queues, automation scripts, payment
integrations, identity workflows, CI/CD systems, or SaaS integrations.

4
00:00:25.574 --> 00:00:31.414
The implementation may look like a small route, but it often sits on top
of important business behavior.

5
00:00:31.414 --> 00:00:34.636
A webhook is different from a normal API request pattern.

6
00:00:34.636 --> 00:00:38.328
With polling, your application asks another system for updates.

7
00:00:38.328 --> 00:00:42.087
With an incoming webhook, another system calls your application.

8
00:00:42.087 --> 00:00:46.853
With an outgoing webhook, your application calls a subscriber when an
event occurs.

9
00:00:46.853 --> 00:00:55.646
Message queues and event streams can provide more durable event delivery
models, but webhooks remain common because HTTP callbacks are easy to
integrate.

10
00:00:55.646 --> 00:00:58.935
Their simplicity is exactly why security design matters.

11
00:00:58.935 --> 00:01:03.768
Webhooks are security-sensitive because they can trigger behavior
automatically.

12
00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:13.165
A single event might create an account, mark an invoice as paid, disable
access, update a ticket, start a build, change a subscription, or notify a
downstream service.

13
00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:19.743
The endpoint receiving the event is often public, and a request reaching
that endpoint is not trustworthy by itself.

14
00:01:19.743 --> 00:01:29.208
The handler must establish source, integrity, freshness, expected event
type, payload shape, authorization context, and business meaning before it
changes state.

15
00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:33.034
Secure webhook design covers more than the first validation check.

16
00:01:33.034 --> 00:01:45.586
It includes source validation, signature verification, shared-secret
protection, payload validation, idempotency, replay resistance, retry-safe
processing, careful logging, monitoring, and incident response.

17
00:01:45.586 --> 00:01:50.755
The best designs centralize these decisions so every handler does not
invent its own rules.

18
00:01:50.755 --> 00:01:57.400
A webhook endpoint should be treated as a small integration system, not as
a casual route that happens to receive JSON.
